Q: Win32 C++ Generic function to set Cursor from either UI or any worker threads (SetCursor does not work from external threads) A: <p>Yes, you should have <code>SetAppCursor()</code> post/send a message<sup>1</sup> to a window in the UI thread, and then have that window handler call <code>SetCursor()</code>, eg:</p>
<p><sup>1</sup>: I would suggest a custom message instead of <code>WM_SETCURSOR</code>.</p>
<pre><code>#define APPWM_SETAPPCURSOR (WM_APP+1)

...

void SetAppCursor(boolean waiting) {
    PostMessage(hWndMain, APPWM_SETAPPCURSOR, (WPARAM)waiting, 0);
}

...

static bool waiting = false;

case APPWM_SETAPPCURSOR:
    waiting = (bool)wParam;
    SetCursor(LoadCursor(NULL, waiting ? IDC_WAIT : IDC_ARROW));
    return 0;

case WM_SETCURSOR:
    if (LOWORD(lParam) == HTCLIENT &amp;&amp; waiting) {
        SetCursor(LoadCursor(NULL, IDC_WAIT));
        return TRUE;
    }
    return DefWindowProc(hWnd, message, wParam, lParam); 
</code></pre>
<pre><code>// Start Waiting Cursor
SetAppCursor(true);
// ... App Logic ...
// Resume Normal Cursor when done
SetAppCursor(false);
</code></pre>
<p>If you don't want to modify an existing window handler, then you can create a dedicated <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/winmsg/window-features#message-only-windows" rel="nofollow noreferrer">message-only window</a> in the UI thread for <code>SetAppCursor()</code> to post/send its messages to.</p>
